        "id": "overlapping-belonging",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Belonging at more than one scale",
        "content": "National and local identities in Iran are best approached as overlapping affiliations. Encyclopaedia Iranica describes kinship, tribal connections, language, religion, and provincial attachments alongside an overarching Iranian identity. Its account also recognizes competition between these allegiances. Shared belonging should therefore not be assumed to remove differences or disagreements. [s1]\n\nThe same essay identifies historical memory and cultural heritage, especially Persian literature, as important sources of cohesion. This is a scholarly interpretation of how a shared identity has been sustained, rather than a measurement of what every Iranian feels. It also describes identity as repeatedly reconstructed through history. A useful reading leaves room for change: attachment to a locality and attachment to a country need not have the same meaning in every period. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "language-and-affiliation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why language is not a complete identity map",
        "content": "Language provides an important connection to a community, but it does not act as a complete inventory of belonging. Gernot Windfuhr's overview distinguishes language, ethnicity, and culture as different, overlapping networks. He illustrates cultural layering through personal names drawn from Iranian, Arabic, Turkic, and European traditions. A name may preserve a historical connection or reflect a new cultural choice without identifying everything about its bearer. [s2]\n\nPlace names introduce another layer. They can retain traces of earlier populations, migrations, or deliberate renaming long after their original meaning becomes obscure. Windfuhr consequently emphasizes careful historical and linguistic analysis. A familiar-sounding name alone is insufficient evidence for claims about the ancestry or present identity of everyone living there. For readers, the practical lesson is to distinguish the history of a word from the self-description of a person. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "shared-practice",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Shared celebrations can cross national boundaries",
        "content": "Nowruz, the celebration marking spring and renewal, offers a concrete example of cultural connections operating across scales. UNESCO describes family meals, visits, new clothing, and gift-giving among its practices. It also places the celebration across a broad geographical area, including the Caucasus, Central Asia, and the Middle East. Participation in such a tradition can connect a household to a wider cultural world without making that world a single nationality. [s3]\n\nUNESCO's account emphasizes intergenerational solidarity and relationships between communities. These are the values highlighted by its heritage description; they should not be read as proof that every celebration has identical customs or achieves the same social outcomes. The example is useful because it shows how a recognizable shared occasion can extend beyond the boundaries of one state. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-difference",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Read identities without turning them into fixed boxes",
        "content": "Taken together, these sources suggest an approach based on context. Ask whether a label concerns language, family history, locality, or a wider historical community. Avoid treating one answer as a substitute for all the others. This is an editorial synthesis of the sources, rather than a survey finding: overlapping affiliations help explain cultural connections, while leaving individuals room to describe the importance of those connections for themselves. [s1][s2][s3]",
