        "heading": "Languages of Iran and Iranian languages",
        "content": "Two similar expressions describe different things. Languages of Iran is a geographical description. Iranian languages is a classification based on descent from a shared ancestral language. Prods Oktor Skjærvø's linguistic overview places the Iranian group within Indo-Iranian, itself a branch of Indo-European. Its members are spoken both inside and far beyond Iran. Persian is one member of this group; the group is not simply a collection of Persian dialects. [s1]\n\nThis distinction helps explain why the language map cannot be reduced to national borders. Kurdish and Balochi, for example, have distributions extending into neighboring countries. Within Iran, Gilaki and Mazandarani belong to the Caspian linguistic landscape, while Luri and Bakhtiari varieties are discussed among the languages of western and southwestern Iran. Talysh is associated with the western Caspian area. These regional descriptions indicate broad distributions, not exclusive territories. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "persian-connections",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Persian connects several literary worlds",
        "content": "Persian has a geographical reach wider than Iran. The Library of Congress uses Persian as the established English name and identifies Farsi, Dari, and Tajiki as regional names. Its exhibition describes a literary culture extending through Central Asia and into South Asia, including settings associated with the Mughal and Ottoman empires. This wider history helps readers understand why Persian texts, authors, and artistic traditions cannot always be assigned exclusively to one modern nation. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "turkic-diversity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Turkic languages have their own internal geography",
        "content": "Turkic languages form another important part of Iran's linguistic landscape. Windfuhr distinguishes the Oghuz varieties from Khalaj, which constitutes a separate Turkic branch. His survey includes Azerbaijani, Qashqai, Khorasan Turkish, and Turkmen, as well as smaller regional varieties. These are not branches of Persian. [s3]\n\nEven a familiar label contains local distinctions. The survey describes Azerbaijani varieties associated with Tabriz, Urmia, Ardabil, and Zanjan. It places Qashqai within southern Oghuz and discusses Turkmen in the northeast. Such distinctions make a broad language label useful as an entry point rather than a complete description of an individual speaker's speech. The classification is linguistic; it should not be treated as a direct statement about a person's nationality or political affiliation. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "other-families",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The landscape extends beyond Iranian and Turkic",
        "content": "Other traditions complicate any two-part picture. Windfuhr's survey identifies Arabic and Neo-Aramaic within the Semitic group, Armenian within Indo-European, Georgian, and Brahui within Dravidian. His historical account also describes Arabic and Turkic influences on Persian and other languages, including vocabulary and grammatical features. Contact is therefore part of the history of the languages themselves, not just an encounter between otherwise isolated communities. [s4]\n\nArabic illustrates why regional detail matters. Windfuhr discusses varieties in Khuzestan and the Persian Gulf region alongside smaller, historically documented communities farther east. He relates Khuzestan Arabic to the dialect continuum of Iraq. Remembered Arab ancestry and continued Arabic speech do not always coincide: his account explicitly describes communities that retained an origin label after language shift. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "multilingual-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Read a language map alongside social context",
        "content": "Windfuhr describes bilingualism and multilingualism as common in many regions and emphasizes intersecting cultural, ethnic, and linguistic affiliations. A map showing a language in a locality consequently does not establish that every resident speaks only that language. It also does not measure proficiency, the language used in a particular setting, or the importance a speaker gives it. Those questions require more specific evidence. [s4]\n\nThe pages in this branch therefore separate major language traditions from the study of local varieties. They offer starting points for understanding relationships and regional distinctions. A responsible overview preserves those distinctions while acknowledging that the sources inspected here do not establish current speaker totals or a complete inventory of every locality. [s1][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4"]
      },
